{
  "book": "GEN",
  "chapter": 45,
  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-0059840d376157bf": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-92",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Wilkinson supposes these wagons to have been similar to the war chariots, but with the sides closed. They had wheels with six spokes, and were drawn by oxen, which were harnessed the same as horses for the war chariots. In traveling the wagon was furnished with a sort of umbrella…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-92",
          "sourceHash": "09e80d3f75544958b4e6e3c498840865228a94c314664441e0cdc94171255b91",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-59e1881c047a4f85": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-93",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Presents of costly and beautiful garments are among the modes of complimenting in use by the Orientals. Since the fashions of dress do not change as with us, these gifts are valuable as long as they last. These “changes of raiment” were designed to he worn on special occasions. O…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-93",
          "sourceHash": "e68e34df6b37019d3ad62db7bcb16908519049a9a0809070dda66d73212b3e8b",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-b469bb7891508c8a": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-91",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "In the East emotions of joy as well as of sorrow are expressed by loud cries. Sir John Chardin (cited by Harmer, Observations, vol. 3, p. 17) says, “Their sentiments of joy or of grief are properly transports; and their transports are ungoverned, excessive, and truly outrageous.”…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-91",
          "sourceHash": "07a2c42b11888eea1303587c6916e777affc2800aea6b61918d8724f5cc908fd",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      }
    },
